%% CASE Voltaris battery plant
Voltaris Motors is converting its former engine plant into a battery cell plant for electric vans. Construction and equipment installation follow a predictive plan; two agile teams build the plant's manufacturing execution software.

- A government **green-industry grant** covers 30% of the cost. It requires quarterly progress reports and that at least 30% of new hires come from the local area.
- The **environmental permit** requires continuous monitoring of air emissions and wastewater. Inspectors can halt work if limits are exceeded.
- **600 employees** from the engine lines will be retrained for battery production. The union is worried about safety in the new environment and about job grades.
- **Lithium prices** have risen 40% in six months, and a new regulation will require a digital **battery passport** (materials and carbon footprint data for each battery) from 2027.
@@ BE2 | Hybrid
How should the project manage the conditions of the environmental permit?
A) Build the monitoring into the plan with clear owners and track compliance regularly
B) Rely on the inspectors to tell the project when one of the permit limits has been exceeded
C) Make the construction contractor solely responsible, since it carries out the work on site
D) Start the monitoring once the plant is producing, since construction emits very little
= A
? Permit conditions are compliance obligations. They are planned, owned and tracked from the start, with evidence kept. Waiting for inspectors or delegating all responsibility leaves the organisation exposed.

@@ BE7 | Hybrid
What is the best way to prepare the 600 employees for the move to battery production?
A) Engage the union and employees early, address safety concerns, and train and support them
B) Announce the new job grades once the plant is ready, to avoid long negotiations with the union
C) Provide a single e-learning course, since experienced workers will adapt quickly
D) Recruit new staff from outside, since retraining long-serving employees takes too long
= A
? Successful organisational change involves the people affected early, addresses their concerns openly and provides training and reinforcement. Surprises and minimal training increase resistance and safety risk.

@@ BE8 | Hybrid
How should the project respond to the rise in lithium prices and the coming battery passport rule?
A) Assess the impact on the business case and plan the passport data needs now
B) Wait until 2027 before considering the battery passport, since the rule does not apply yet
C) Fix all lithium purchases for ten years at today's price, whatever the contract terms are
D) Continue as planned, since the board approved the scope and the budget last year
= A
? External changes are analysed for their impact on value, cost and requirements. The passport affects the software and data design, so it should be planned now rather than retrofitted later.

@@ BE5 | Hybrid
The risk register includes: "Emissions could exceed permit limits during commissioning, and inspectors could halt work." Which response is most appropriate?
A) Mitigate it with monitoring, alarms and a tested procedure to stop the process quickly
B) Accept it, since inspectors rarely halt work at newly built plants that are still being commissioned
C) Transfer it to an insurer, which prevents any stoppage from occurring
D) Avoid it by commissioning the plant without testing the emission controls first
= A
? Mitigation reduces the probability and impact of the threat. Insurance can transfer some financial impact, but it cannot prevent a stoppage; skipping tests increases the risk.
%% END CASE

@@ BE1 | Predictive
The governance plan lets the project manager handle forecast cost variances of up to 5%; larger variances go to the steering committee. The forecast now shows a 7% overrun. What should the project manager do?
A) Escalate to the steering committee with an analysis and options
B) Cover the overrun from the contingency reserve without telling the committee, since reserves exist for this
C) Wait until the overrun actually occurs, since a forecast may still change before the end
D) Reduce scope quietly until the forecast is back within the 5% tolerance
= A
? Tolerances define when decisions move to a higher level of governance. A forecast beyond tolerance is escalated with analysis and options so that the committee can decide.

@@ BE1 | Hybrid
Team members have started pasting customer contracts into a public generative AI tool to summarise them. What should the project manager do first?
A) Check the organisation's AI and data policies and agree how the tool may be used
B) Ban all AI tools on the project permanently, since AI outputs may contain errors
C) Let each team member decide how to use the tool, since it saves a lot of time
D) Ask the AI tool's vendor to confirm in writing that the contract data will be safe
= A
? Using AI on a project needs governance: policies on confidential data, approved tools and human review of outputs. Confidential contract data must not be shared with a public tool without approval.

@@ BE1 | Agile
A product owner cannot make decisions without approval from three different managers, so the team often waits days for answers. What should the agile coach recommend?
A) Agree the product owner's decision authority and escalation path with the sponsor
B) Let the developers make product decisions whenever the product owner is unavailable
C) Ask the three managers to attend every daily scrum so that they can answer sooner
D) Accept the delays, since several approvals reduce the risk of making a wrong decision
= A
? Agile delivery depends on fast decisions by an empowered product owner. Governance should define the product owner's decision rights and when to escalate.

@@ BE1 | Predictive
At a phase gate, the design is complete, but the business case has weakened because market demand has fallen. What should the governance board do?
A) Decide whether to continue, change or stop, based on the updated business case
B) Approve the next phase automatically, since the design phase finished on time
C) Let the project continue while the board waits to see whether demand recovers next year
D) Postpone the gate review until the business case looks stronger
= A
? A phase gate is a decision point. The board uses performance information and the updated business case to continue, change direction or terminate the project.

@@ BE1 | Hybrid
An insurer's hybrid programme has a steering committee made up only of business executives, and major technical risks in the platform work are often missed. What should be done?
A) Add technical expertise to governance, for example an architect or a technical review board
B) Ask the agile teams to stop reporting technical risks, since the committee cannot assess them
C) Replace the business executives with technical staff so that technical risks are understood
D) Hold the steering committee meetings less often so that more time is left for technical work
= A
? Governance bodies need the right expertise to oversee the work. Adding technical input improves decisions without removing business accountability.

@@ BE1 | Agile
An innovation project is funded in stages. Each stage receives money only if the previous stage produced validated learning about customers. What is this governance approach called?
A) Metered funding released in stages
B) A fixed annual budget approved in advance
C) A cost-reimbursable contract with the team
D) A management reserve held by the sponsor
= A
? Metered funding releases money in increments based on evidence, which limits the investment at risk while uncertainty is high.

@@ BE2 | Predictive
A new packaging line for a pharmaceutical company must comply with good manufacturing practice (GMP) rules. When should these requirements be identified?
A) During planning, so that they shape the requirements, design and tests
B) Before the final inspection, when the line is ready to be validated
C) After the first audit finding, so that effort goes only where needed
D) Only if the regulator asks for them during an inspection visit
= A
? Compliance requirements are identified early so that they are built into requirements, design, quality activities and acceptance criteria. Late discovery causes expensive rework.

@@ BE2 | Agile
How can an agile team in a regulated industry produce the documentation that auditors need without slowing delivery?
A) Produce documentation incrementally as part of the definition of done
B) Write all of the documentation at the end, once the product is stable
C) Skip the documentation, since agile values working software over documents
D) Ask the auditors to accept verbal explanations from the developers instead
= A
? Making required documentation part of the definition of done keeps it current and avoids a large, risky documentation effort at the end. Agile values do not remove regulatory obligations.

@@ BE2 | Hybrid
A new marketing app will collect customers' location data. What should the project do?
A) Involve the data protection officer and complete a privacy impact assessment early
B) Collect the data now and ask for customers' consent later if anyone complains
C) Rely on the app store's review process to confirm that the app meets privacy law
D) Anonymise the data only after launch, once it is clear which data is really useful
= A
? Personal data must be handled lawfully from the start. Early involvement of privacy experts and an impact assessment build privacy into the design.

@@ BE2 | Predictive
An internal audit finds that safety inspections on the project were not documented for three months. What should the project manager do?
A) Correct the gap, find the root cause and add controls to prevent recurrence
B) Recreate the missing inspection records with past dates to close the finding
C) Tell the auditor the inspections were done, even though they were not recorded
D) Take no action, since no accident occurred during the three months
= A
? Audit findings are addressed by corrective action and preventive controls. Back-dating records is falsification and a serious ethical breach.

@@ BE2 | Hybrid
The project uses a subcontractor in another country, where labour laws differ from those in the home country. What should the project manager do?
A) Require compliance with the applicable local laws in the contract and verify it
B) Apply only the home country's labour laws, since the contract is signed there
C) Leave the matter to the subcontractor, since it employs its own workers
D) Avoid foreign subcontractors in future, since laws differ between countries
= A
? The project must ensure that work is done lawfully wherever it takes place. Contract terms and verification (for example, audits) provide assurance.

@@ BE3 | Predictive
Who can submit a change request on a predictive project?
A) Any stakeholder, through the change control process
B) Only the sponsor, since the sponsor funds the project
C) Only the project manager, since the PM owns the plan
D) Only members of the change control board
= A
? Any stakeholder may request a change. All requests go through the change control process, where they are analysed and decided by the appropriate authority.

@@ BE3 | Agile
A senior executive asks a developer directly to add a feature in the middle of a sprint, which would put the sprint goal at risk. What should happen?
A) The request goes to the product owner, who decides where it fits in the product backlog
B) The developer adds the feature at once, since the request comes from a senior executive
C) The scrum master rejects the request permanently to protect the team from interruptions
D) The team cancels the sprint immediately and starts a new sprint that includes the feature
= A
? The product owner manages the product backlog. New requests are ordered against other work, usually for a future sprint, so that the sprint goal is protected.

@@ BE3 | Hybrid | match
Match each change with who would normally decide on it in a hybrid project.
1) A change to the approved budget baseline
2) Reordering items in the product backlog
3) A change to the team's working agreements
4) A change to the strategic objectives the project supports
A) The change control board
B) The product owner
C) The team itself
D) Portfolio or executive governance
= 1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D
? Decision rights are tailored by level: baseline changes go through formal change control, backlog order is the product owner's decision, working agreements belong to the team, and strategy belongs to senior governance.

@@ BE3 | Predictive
A proposed change to the claims system's data model would affect reports owned by the finance department. What should happen before the change control board decides?
A) Finance is included in the impact analysis
B) The change is approved first, and finance is informed afterwards
C) The development team decides whether finance needs to know
D) Finance is asked to rewrite its reports before the request is submitted
= A
? Impact analysis covers everyone affected by a change, so that the board decides with full information and affected stakeholders can prepare.

@@ BE3 | Predictive
A customer requests a change that would add three weeks of work. What should the project manager do before the change control board meets?
A) Analyse the impact on scope, schedule, cost, quality and risk, and prepare options
B) Tell the customer that changes are not allowed once the baseline has been approved
C) Ask the team to absorb the extra work so that the end date does not move
D) Start the work, since the customer is paying for the project
= A
? The board needs an impact analysis and options to make an informed decision. Absorbing or starting unapproved work bypasses change control.

@@ BE3 | Agile | multi
Which two practices help an agile team respond to change effectively?
A) Short iterations with frequent reviews of the product
B) A product backlog that is refined continuously
C) A frozen requirements specification for the release
D) Board approval for every new user story added
E) A detailed design of the whole product created upfront
= A, B
? Frequent feedback and a continuously refined backlog let the team adapt quickly. Frozen specifications, heavy approvals and big upfront design make change slow and expensive.

@@ BE4 | Agile
A vendor's interface keeps returning errors in the test environment, and the vendor is slow to respond. The team's integration work is blocked. What should the scrum master do?
A) Escalate through the vendor management channel, while the team uses a mock service to continue
B) Wait for the vendor to fix the interface before doing any more work on the integration
C) Ask the developers to fix the vendor's interface themselves to save time
D) Remove the integration stories from the sprint and from the product backlog
= A
? The scrum master pursues the impediment through the right channel, while the team reduces the impact, for example by working against a mock service.

@@ BE4 | Predictive
An issue threatens a key milestone, and its solution is beyond the project manager's authority. What should the project manager do?
A) Escalate it to the sponsor with an analysis and options, as defined in governance
B) Keep working on it within the team until the milestone is actually missed
C) Leave it out of the status report until a solution has been found
D) Record it in the lessons learned register so that future projects can avoid the same issue
= A
? Issues beyond the project manager's authority are escalated through the agreed governance path, with analysis and options, so that decisions can be made in time.

@@ BE4 | Hybrid
The organisation's purchasing process takes six weeks, even for low-cost software licences, and the agile teams are often blocked. What should the project manager do?
A) Agree a faster route for low-value, low-risk purchases with procurement
B) Buy the licences with personal credit cards and claim them as expenses afterwards
C) Tell the teams to stop work on any item that needs a new licence until it arrives
D) Ignore the purchasing rules for small amounts, since they slow down the project
= A
? Systemic impediments are solved by working with the function involved to improve the process, within the organisation's rules.

@@ BE4 | Agile
Impediments raised in daily scrums are often forgotten by the following week. What should the scrum master introduce?
A) A visible impediment board, with an owner for each item, reviewed regularly
B) A rule that impediments may be raised only at retrospectives, so that daily scrums stay short
C) A monthly report of open impediments sent to senior management for their information
D) A requirement for team members to email impediments to the scrum master privately
= A
? Making impediments visible, with clear owners and regular review, keeps them from being forgotten and supports transparency.

@@ BE4 | Predictive
A shipment of critical equipment is delayed by a strike at the port, and the delay affects the critical path. What should the project manager do first?
A) Assess the impact and options, such as other ports, routes or resequencing work
B) Terminate the supplier's contract, since the equipment has not arrived on the agreed date
C) Wait for the strike to end, since it is outside the project's control
D) Ask the team to work overtime immediately to recover the lost time
= A
? An issue is first analysed for impact and options. Termination is not justified by a strike, and overtime may not address the real constraint.

@@ BE4 | Hybrid
An identified risk has occurred. What should happen in the project's logs?
A) It is recorded as an issue, and the planned response is put into action
B) It stays in the risk register only, since it was identified as a risk earlier
C) It is removed from both the risk register and the issue log once it has occurred
D) It is recorded in the lessons learned register only, for future projects
= A
? When a risk occurs, it becomes an issue: the issue log is updated, the planned response is implemented, and the risk register is updated too.

@@ BE5 | Predictive | fill
Risk 1 has a 20% probability and an impact of $100,000. Risk 2 has a 10% probability and an impact of $250,000. What contingency reserve, based on expected monetary value, covers both? (Answer in dollars.)
= $45,000
? EMV = 0.20 × 100,000 + 0.10 × 250,000 = 20,000 + 25,000 = $45,000.

@@ BE5 | Agile
A team is unsure whether a new integration technology can meet its performance needs. What is the most effective way to reduce this risk?
A) Run a spike early and deliver the riskiest items first, to learn quickly
B) Leave the integration until the end, when more information will be available
C) Add the risk to the register and review it at the end of the release
D) Ask the vendor to guarantee that the technology will perform well enough
= A
? Agile teams reduce risk by learning early: spikes and early delivery of high-risk items expose problems while there is still time to change direction.

@@ BE5 | Hybrid
The sponsor states that the organisation will accept a delay of no more than two weeks to the product launch. What is this statement an example of?
A) A risk threshold
B) A risk trigger
C) A risk response
D) A residual risk
= A
? A risk threshold is the level of variation an organisation is willing to accept for an objective. It guides when risks need a response or escalation.

@@ BE5 | Predictive
Who should be assigned as the owner of a risk?
A) The person best able to monitor and manage it
B) The most senior person on the project
C) The person who first identified the risk
D) The project manager, for every risk
= A
? Each risk is assigned to the person best placed to monitor it and manage its response, who may not be the project manager.

@@ BE5 | Predictive
A new test tool could cut testing time by 30%. The project manager assigns the organisation's most experienced testers to make sure this benefit is achieved. Which opportunity response is this?
A) Exploit
B) Enhance
C) Share
D) Accept
= A
? Exploiting an opportunity tries to make sure it happens, for example by assigning the best resources. Enhancing only increases its probability or impact.

@@ BE5 | Agile | multi
Which two signs show that an agile team manages risk well?
A) Risk responses appear as items in the product backlog
B) Risks are discussed in planning, reviews and retrospectives
C) Risks are reviewed once a year by the PMO only
D) Only the scrum master is allowed to know about the risks
E) The risk list is kept private to avoid alarming stakeholders
= A, B
? In agile work, risk management is continuous and transparent: risk responses are prioritised in the backlog, and risks are discussed at every cadence event.

@@ BE6 | Agile
One agile team halved its cycle time by adopting a new practice. How can the other teams benefit?
A) Share it through a community of practice and let each team experiment and adapt it
B) Require all teams to adopt the practice exactly as the first team implemented it
C) Ask the first team to keep the practice to itself, since its context is unique
D) Wait until the practice has been used for a year before sharing it with others
= A
? Improvements spread best when they are shared openly and other teams can experiment with them in their own context.

@@ BE6 | Predictive
When should lessons learned be captured on a predictive project?
A) Throughout the project, at regular points and after key events
B) Only at closure, when the full picture of the project is available
C) Only when something has gone wrong, to avoid repeating the mistake
D) Only when the sponsor or the PMO asks for them to be documented
= A
? Capturing lessons throughout the project lets the project itself benefit and avoids losing details by the end.

@@ BE6 | Hybrid
Small purchases on the project need twelve signatures, and most of them add no value. Which lean concept applies?
A) Removing waste in the form of steps that add no value
B) Gold plating, by adding extra approval steps to the process
C) Crashing, by adding more people to the approval process
D) Fast tracking, by collecting the signatures in a different order
= A
? Lean thinking identifies and removes non-value-adding steps (waste) to improve flow.

@@ BE6 | Predictive
Which improvement method follows the steps define, measure, analyse, improve and control?
A) Six Sigma (DMAIC)
B) Kaizen events
C) Value stream mapping
D) Kanban flow management
= A
? DMAIC is the Six Sigma improvement cycle. Kaizen focuses on small continuous improvements; Scrum and Kanban are delivery frameworks and methods.

@@ BE6 | Agile
Which measure would best show whether a team's process improvements are making delivery faster?
A) A falling trend in the team's cycle time
B) The number of meetings held each sprint
C) The total hours worked by the team each week
D) The number of lines of code written per sprint
= A
? Cycle time measures how long work takes from start to finish, so a falling trend shows faster delivery. Activity measures do not show outcomes.

@@ BE7 | Hybrid
The insurer's new agile teams are ready, but the human resources department still rewards only individual performance, which discourages teamwork. What should be done?
